HST PDF: selected text, search matches and the signature box stay readable on Windows.

On Windows, selecting text in HST PDF painted solid dark navy bars that hid the selected words. The
search matches and the signature placement box had the same fault: dark olive and near-black boxes.
macOS and Linux showed the intended light tint.

The viewer tinted each box with OutputDevice::DrawTransparent(tools::PolyPolygon, percent). On
Windows only, that overload sends a rectangle to the emulation path, which calls DrawAlphaRect, to
avoid GDI+ dithering on remote displays. Skia's drawAlphaRect fills with the "source" blend mode, so
it writes the colour with its alpha into the window surface instead of blending it over the page.
The screen then shows the premultiplied colour: 35% of the selection blue over black, the
(20, 46, 83) of the report, and no text. Other platforms take the native path, which blends.

The viewer now tints with the basegfx overload of DrawTransparent. That overload draws the polygon
with the backend's blending fill on every platform: Skia on Windows, macOS and Linux, GDI+, cairo and
Quartz. The colours and their transparency are unchanged: the selection blue at 65% transparency,
the search yellow and orange at 55%, the signature green at 93%. Black text under the selection
stays dark, on a light blue background.

tests/runtime/check-pdf-selection.py (Linux, Xvfb) and check-windows-pdf-selection.py (the Windows
smoke) select text with the real mouse and check the screen: the highlight stays light and the
selected glyphs stay much darker than it.

